  {
    "parts": [
      {
        "content": "## Summary\n\n**Target Fact:** `F:ml430-nat-modeq-one-516d46e8`  \n**Formal Statement:** `∀ {a b : ℕ}, a ≡ b [MOD 1]`\n\n**Structural Shape:**  \nThis is a universal statement asserting that any two natural numbers are congruent modulo 1. The goal is a modular equivalence relation (`modEq`) with modulus 1, applied to arbitrary naturals `a` and `b`.\n\n**Dependencies:**  \n- No dependencies (`depends_on: []`)\n- Would unlock nothing (`would_unlock: []`)\n- No registered operations target this fact\n\n**Available Premises:**  \nThe Nat prelude contains only one `modEq` theorem: `Nat.modEq_pow`, which relates modular equivalence of powers. There are 17 theorems mentioning \"one\", including `Nat.mul_one`, `Nat.one_mul`, `Nat.one_pow`, and various properties about divisibility and ordering with 1.\n\n**Similar Eligible Facts:**  \nThe frontier contains 98 eligible facts, many involving modular equivalence:\n- `F:ml430-nat-modeq-refl-d870c8f5` (reflexivity)\n- `F:ml430-nat-modeq-symm-0a3d4d18` (symmetry)\n- `F:ml430-nat-modeq-trans-ef9d1c46` (transitivity)\n- `F:ml430-nat-modeq-of-dvd-d75cc374` (from divisibility)\n- `F:ml430-nat-modeq-add-left-e83f0700`, `F:ml430-nat-modeq-add-right-8e2ca0cc` (congruence properties)\n- Similar Int fragment modeq facts\n\nThe modEq-one fact appears to be a trivial consequence of the definition (everything divides everything modulo 1), but lacks direct kernel support for the modEq relation itself.",
        "id": null,
        "provider_name": null,
        "provider_details": null,
        "part_kind": "text"
      }
    ],
